1
00:06:47,500 --> 00:06:53,500
For passion. For honor. For destiny. For victory. For love.

2
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:41,000
Troy is a war, action, history film directed by Wolfgang Petersen.

3
00:20:22,500 --> 00:20:28,500
In year 1250 B.C.

4
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:16,000
during the late Bronze age, two emerging nations begin to clash.

5
00:33:57,500 --> 00:34:03,500
Paris, the Trojan prince, convinces Helen, Queen of Sparta, to leave
her husband Menelaus, and sail with him back to Troy.

6
00:40:45,000 --> 00:40:51,000
After Menelaus finds out that his wife was taken by the Trojans, he
asks his brother Agamemnon to help him get her back.

7
00:47:32,500 --> 00:47:38,500
Agamemnon sees this as an opportunity for power.

8
00:54:20,000 --> 00:54:26,000
They set off with 1,000 ships holding 50,000 Greeks to Troy.

9
01:01:07,500 --> 01:01:13,500
Achilles, played by Brad Pitt.

10
01:07:55,000 --> 01:08:01,000
Paris, played by Orlando Bloom.

11
01:14:42,500 --> 01:14:48,500
Hector, played by Eric Bana.

12
01:21:30,000 --> 01:21:36,000
King Agamemnon, played by Brian Cox.

13
01:28:17,500 --> 01:28:23,500
Odysseus, played by Sean Bean.

14
01:35:05,000 --> 01:35:11,000
Menelaos, played by Brendan Gleeson.

15
01:41:52,500 --> 01:41:58,500
Troy is a 2004 epic historical action film directed by
Wolfgang Petersen and written by David Benioff.

16
01:48:40,000 --> 01:48:46,000
Produced by units in Malta, Mexico and Britain's Shepperton Studios, the film features an ensemble cast led by Brad Pitt,
Eric Bana, Sean Bean, Diane Kruger, Brian Cox, Brendan Gleeson, Rose Byrne, Saffron Burrows and Orlando Bloom.

17
01:55:27,500 --> 01:55:33,500
It is loosely based on Homer's Iliad in its narration of the entire story of the decade-long Trojan War—condensed into little
more than a couple of weeks, rather than just the quarrel between Achilles and Agamemnon in the ninth year.

18
02:02:15,000 --> 02:02:21,000
Achilles leads his Myrmidons along with the rest of the Greek army invading
the historical city of Troy, defended by Hector's Trojan army.

19
02:09:02,500 --> 02:09:08,500
The end of the film (the sack of Troy) is not taken from the Iliad, but rather from Quintus
Smyrnaeus's Posthomerica, as the Iliad concludes with Hector's death and funeral.

20
02:15:50,000 --> 02:15:56,000
Troy made over $497 million worldwide, making it the 60th highest-grossing film
at the time of its release and Petersen's highest-grossing film.

21
02:22:37,500 --> 02:22:43,500
However, it received mixed reviews, with critics praising its entertainment value and the performances
of Pitt and Bana  while criticizing its story, which was deemed unfaithful to the Iliad.

22
02:29:25,000 --> 02:29:31,000
It received a nomination for Best Costume Design at the 77th Academy
Awards and was the eighth highest-grossing film of 2004.

23
02:36:12,500 --> 02:36:18,500
The film explores themes of adultery, sibling
relationship, hostility, bravery, mythology.
